`skoro-dense` is host-resident linear algebra in `f64`, backed by [faer](https://faer.veganb.tw/).
It depends only on `skoro-backend` and `skoro-core`, so nothing that merely uses `Normal` pays for
`faer`. It is independent of everything on the previous pages. They produce its inputs, and the
next pages consume its outputs.

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-dense/src/lib.rs:1..4}
```

## Dense

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-dense/src/lib.rs:8..14}
```

`skoro_dense::factor` has the dense entry points, all returning `SkoroResult`: `cholesky`,
`solve_lower`, `solve_upper`, `spd_solve`, `lstsq`, and `whitening_from_precision`. The last
returns, for a precision $\Lam$, the **lower**-triangular $W$ with $W\transpose W = \Lam$ that a
`Tril` whitening needs. It uses one Cholesky and no inversion. Let $P$ reverse the order of
coordinates, and factor $P\Lam P = LL\transpose$ with $L$ lower. Then $W = P L\transpose P$ is
lower-triangular (reversing both axes of an upper-triangular matrix makes it lower), and
$$
W\transpose W = P L P\, P L\transpose P = P (P\Lam P) P = \Lam .
$$
($\mathrm{chol}(\Lam)\transpose$ also squares to $\Lam$, but it is upper-triangular, which a `Tril`
cannot hold.)

## Why QR on the host

A damped Gauss–Newton step solves
$$
(J\transpose J + \lambda I)\,\delta = -J\transpose r
\iff
\min_\delta \left\lVert \begin{bmatrix} J \\ \sqrt\lambda\, I \end{bmatrix}\delta - \begin{bmatrix} -r \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\rVert .
$$
Solving the right-hand form by QR keeps the conditioning at $\kappa(J)$ rather than
$\kappa(J)^2$, at *every* damping value. burn has no QR, and that settles where the solve runs.
Everything on the rest of this page is a sparse form of that QR.

> [!DECISION]
> See [Solves on the host](../../design/host-dense-linear-algebra/).

## Factor graphs

A model's linearization is block-sparse: each term touches a few quantities. `skoro-dense`
represents it with keys, not scalars, as the unit, as GTSAM does:

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-dense/src/lib.rs:18..33}
```

A `GaussianFactorGraph` declares keys $k$ with dimensions $d_k$, and factors $f$, each a block of
rows over a few keys $K_f$:
$$
\Phi(\delta) = \sum_f \tfrac12 \big\lVert A_f\,\delta_{K_f} - b_f \big\rVert^2 = \tfrac12\lVert A\delta - b\rVert^2 .
$$
Here $A$ stacks the factors' rows, each scattered into the columns of its keys. The curvature of
$\Phi$ is $\Lam = A\transpose A$. The graph never asks where the rows came from. Whitened residual
Jacobians with $b = -r$, Fisher roots with $b = 0$, and damping priors are all the same object
(see [Linearization and marginalization](../linearization/)).

## Eliminating one key

Pick a key $j$. Only the factors touching $j$ involve $\delta_j$. Stack them into one dense matrix
over $j$ and the other keys they touch, its **separator** $\sep_j$, with $\delta_j$'s columns first:
$$
M = \big[\, A_j \;\big|\; A_{\sep} \;\big|\; b \,\big].
$$
An orthogonal $Q$ (Householder QR, **unpivoted**) brings it to upper-trapezoidal form:
$$
Q\transpose M = \begin{bmatrix} R_j & T & d_j \\ 0 & A'_{\sep} & b' \\ 0 & 0 & e \end{bmatrix},
$$
where $R_j$ is square upper-triangular. Since $Q$ preserves norms, those factors' contribution to
$\Phi$ splits exactly:
$$
\tfrac12\lVert A_j\delta_j + A_\sep\delta_\sep - b\rVert^2
= \underbrace{\tfrac12\lVert R_j\delta_j + T\delta_\sep - d_j\rVert^2}_{\text{conditional on } \delta_\sep}
+ \underbrace{\tfrac12\lVert A'_\sep\delta_\sep - b'\rVert^2}_{\text{new factor on } \sep}
+ \tfrac12 e^2 .
$$

- The first term is a **conditional**: for any $\delta_\sep$ it is zero at
  $\delta_j = R_j^{-1}(d_j - T\delta_\sep)$, when $R_j$ is invertible. So minimizing over
  $\delta_j$ removes it.
- The second is a new factor on the separator. It replaces every factor that touched $j$.
- $\tfrac12 e^2$ is residual error that no choice of $\delta$ can remove.

**Why this is the Schur complement.** Write $\Lam$ restricted to $(j, \sep)$ in blocks. Because
$Q$ is orthogonal, $M\transpose M = (Q\transpose M)\transpose(Q\transpose M)$, which gives
$\Lam_{jj} = R_j\transpose R_j$, $\Lam_{j\sep} = R_j\transpose T$ and
$\Lam_{\sep\sep} = T\transpose T + A'^{\top}_{\sep} A'_{\sep}$. Hence
$$
\Lam_{\sep\sep} - \Lam_{\sep j}\Lam_{jj}^{-1}\Lam_{j\sep}
= T\transpose T + A'^{\top}_\sep A'_\sep - T\transpose R_j (R_j\transpose R_j)^{-1} R_j\transpose T
= A'^{\top}_\sep A'_\sep .
$$
The new factor is a square root of the Schur complement, reached without forming $\Lam$ or
inverting anything.

Column pivoting would be wrong here, not merely different: it would mix $\delta_j$'s columns with
the separator's, and the partition is the whole point. A diagonal of $R_j$ below
$10^{-12}$ of its column's norm means $\delta_j$ is not determined by the rows that reach it: gauge
freedom, or a direction no factor informs. It is reported as `RankDeficient { position }`. A key
no factor touches is `KeyUnconstrained`. The threshold assumes rows computed in `f64`. Rows that
passed through `f32` carry $10^{-7}$ relative noise, and an exact dependence then survives the test
and comes back as a huge step.

## Eliminating all of them: order, trees and fronts

Eliminate keys one at a time in an order $\pi$. Each elimination adds a factor on its separator,
which later eliminations must absorb. How big those separators grow (the **fill**) is decided
entirely by the order.

**Ordering.** `Ordering::Colamd` runs faer's COLAMD on the **block** pattern, one row per factor,
one column per key, and one entry per $(f, k)$ with $k \in K_f$. Every scalar column of a key shares
its sparsity, so ordering keys is cheaper and loses nothing. `ConstrainedLast(K)` orders every
other key by COLAMD and puts $K$ last, which is what a marginal on $K$ needs. It stands in for the
CCOLAMD that faer lacks.

**The elimination tree.** The separators depend only on which keys each factor touches, so they are
computed before any number is. Home each factor at its earliest key in $\pi$. Then, in order,
$$
\sep_j = \Big(\bigcup_{f \text{ homed at } j} K_f \;\cup \bigcup_{c:\ \mathrm{parent}(c) = j} \sep_c\Big) \setminus \{j\},
\qquad \mathrm{parent}(j) = \min_\pi \sep_j .
$$
The parent of $j$ is the first key its new factor lands on.

**The junction tree.** A chain in which a key $j$ has a single child $c$ with
$\sep_c = \{j\} \cup \sep_j$ can be eliminated as one dense front with no extra fill. Such chains
are merged into **cliques**, each with frontal keys $F$ and separator $\sep$. The `JunctionTree`
holds the cliques, children before parents. It holds no numbers, and it is reused for as long as
the graph's `Structure` (each factor's keys, each key's dimension) is unchanged: across solver
iterations, the rows of a batch, and filter ticks of the same topology. Reuse is checked against
the structure, never trusted, and never keyed on the graph epoch, which a window bumps every tick.
`JunctionTree::sequential` turns merging off, and serves as the baseline the multifrontal form is
tested against.

**Multifrontal elimination.** Each clique stacks its homed factors and its children's separator
factors into $[\,A_F \mid A_\sep \mid b\,]$ and eliminates all its frontals with one QR, exactly as
for one key. The top rows become a **conditional**
$$
R_c\,\delta_{F} + S_c\,\delta_{\sep} = d_c ,
$$
with $R_c$ upper-triangular, its diagonal made positive by flipping row signs (which changes no
norm). The next rows are the clique's separator factor, handed to its parent.

## The Bayes tree, and what it solves

The conditionals, arranged as the cliques were, form a `BayesTree`. After a full elimination, the
block rows $[\,R_c \ S_c\,]$ stacked in elimination order are one upper-triangular $R$ with
$$
R\transpose R = A\transpose A = \Lam, \qquad \Phi(\delta) = \tfrac12\lVert R\delta - d\rVert^2 + \text{error}.
$$
Everything follows from triangular solves with $R$, one clique at a time:

| Method | Computes | Direction | Use |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `back_substitute` | $R^{-1}d$ | top-down, $\delta_F = R_c^{-1}(d_c - S_c\delta_\sep)$ | the minimizer of $\Phi$: the least-squares step |
| `solve_upper(z)` | $R^{-1}z$ | top-down | $z \sim \mathcal N(0, I)$ gives $\delta \sim \mathcal N(0, \Lam^{-1})$: Laplace draws |
| `solve_upper_transpose(g)` | $R^{-\top}g$ | bottom-up | the first half of the next row |
| `solve_normal(g)` | $R^{-1}R^{-\top}g = \Lam^{-1}g$ | both | a step against an external gradient (Fisher scoring) |
| `log_det_r` | $\sum \ln R_{ii}$ | | $\tfrac12\ln\det\Lam$, for a Laplace evidence |

`Elimination::error` is $\tfrac12\sum e^2 = \min_\delta \Phi(\delta)$, the predicted objective
a Levenberg–Marquardt gain ratio compares against.

`solve_normal` is sensitive at $\kappa(A)^2$, as any normal-equations solve is, even though $R$ was
computed at $\kappa(A)$. `back_substitute` avoids that whenever the rows carry their own right-hand
side.

**Damping** is two more rows per key. `damped(λ)` appends $\sqrt\lambda\,I$ with $b = 0$, and
`damped_diagonal(λ, D)` appends $\sqrt\lambda\,D^{1/2}$, which turns $\Lam$ into
$\Lam + \lambda D$ at no cost in conditioning.

## Marginals are partial elimination

Split the keys into eliminated $E$ and kept $K$. Eliminating only $E$ leaves a set of factors on
$K$: the factors that touched no key of $E$, untouched, plus the separator factors of the last
eliminated cliques. By the one-key argument, applied repeatedly,
$$
\min_{\delta_E} \Phi(\delta_E, \delta_K) = \Phi_K(\delta_K) + \text{error}, \qquad
\nabla^2 \Phi_K = \Lam_{KK} - \Lam_{KE}\Lam_{EE}^{-1}\Lam_{EK} .
$$
That is the Laplace marginal on $K$ in square-root form. For a linear-Gaussian model it is exact.
Elimination here is lazy: only the factors that touch $E$ are read. The cost is in the eliminated
keys' neighbourhood, not in the size of the graph.

`marginal(graph, K)` eliminates everything else (ordered by `ConstrainedLast(K)`) and **squares up**
what is left into one lower-triangular $W$:
$$
\Phi_K(\delta_K) = \tfrac12\lVert W\delta_K - d\rVert^2 + \text{const}, \qquad W\transpose W = \Lam_K .
$$
QR gives an upper factor, so the last QR runs on the columns reversed. With $P$ the reversal, QR of
$[\,A_K P \mid b\,]$ gives an upper $R'$ with $R'^{\top}R' = P\Lam_K P$ and right-hand side $d'$.
Then $W = P R' P$ is lower with $W\transpose W = \Lam_K$, and since
$A_K\delta = (A_K P)(P\delta)$,
$$
\lVert R' (P\delta) - d' \rVert = \lVert P R' P\,\delta - P d'\rVert = \lVert W\delta - Pd'\rVert, \qquad d = P d' .
$$
A `Marginal` carries its keys (in the caller's order) *with* $W$, $d$, $\ln\det W = \tfrac12\ln\det\Lam_K$,
the information $W\transpose W$, the covariance $W^{-1}W^{-\top}$, and the mean offset $W^{-1}d$.
Fewer rows than $\sum_{k \in K} d_k$ means a singular marginal, `RankDeficient`.

### Marginalizing a key *out*

A sliding window needs something subtly different: not the marginal of the whole graph on some
keys, but the factor that **replaces** the factors of the keys it retires. Split
$\Phi = \Phi_{\ni E} + \Phi_{\not\ni E}$ into the factors that touch $E$ and those that do not, and
let $B$ be the other keys the first group touches, $E$'s **Markov blanket**. Since
$\Phi_{\not\ni E}$ does not involve $\delta_E$,
$$
\min_{\delta_E} \Phi = \underbrace{\min_{\delta_E} \Phi_{\ni E}(\delta_E, \delta_B)}_{\varphi_B(\delta_B)} + \Phi_{\not\ni E} .
$$
So replacing $\Phi_{\ni E}$ by $\varphi_B$ leaves the problem on the remaining keys unchanged.
`marginalize_out(graph, E)` computes exactly $\varphi_B$, from the factors that touch $E$ and
nothing else. `marginal(graph, B)` would compute
$\min_{\delta_{\neq B}} \Phi$ instead, folding in $\Phi_{\not\ni E}$ as well. Attached as a factor
while the model keeps $\Phi_{\not\ni E}$, that would count those factors twice.

## Keeping two triangular solves apart

`TrilInvert` (in `skoro-linalg`) is a differentiable graph operation on every backend, and it stays
there. `skoro-dense`'s triangular solve is for estimators. A shared test checks that the two compute
the same inverse.
